from cap import Fill
c = Fill(37, "M02")
S = lambda key, text, lines, **kw: c.f(key, kw.pop("kind", "statement"), text, lines, **kw)
S("neg", "No text, as searched, speaks of God's own heart, spirit or soul being hardened, made heavy or made stubborn, whether by God himself or by anyone else; the nearest case is Isa 27:8, a hardening word of his breath, which is the east wind as his instrument, and the one refusal in his own seat is Jer 15:1.", [22], kind="silence", verses=["Isa.27.8", "Jer.15.1"], flag="the answer paragraphs, lines 9-20, which name no verse")
S("t41", "Exo 7:14's \"hardened\" is the adjective kaved (H3515), found after the first reading.", [41], kind="term", verses=["Exod.7.14"])
S("gh1", "God hardens a human seat (19 verses): Pharaoh (Exo 4:21; 7:3; 9:12; 10:1, 20, 27; 11:10; 14:4, 8) and the Egyptians (14:17); Sihon, \"the Lord your God hardened his spirit and made his heart obstinate\" (Deu 2:30); the Canaanites (Jos 11:20); \"Make the heart of this people dull [heavy]\" (Isa 6:10); \"why do you ... harden our heart?\" (Isa 63:17); Joh 12:40; Rom 9:18; \"I gave them over to their stubborn hearts\" (Psa 81:12).", list(range(46, 53)))
S("gh2", "The one positive case: God makes the prophet's face and forehead hard against a hard people (Eze 3:8-9).", [53])
S("hh", "A human seat is hardened by the person, or with no agent named (68 verses): Pharaoh by himself (Exo 8:15, 32; 9:34); the stiff neck (Exo 32:9 ... Neh 9:29; Jer 7:26; 17:23; 19:15); sherirut, \"the stubbornness of their evil heart\" (Jer 3:17; 7:24; 9:14; 11:8; 13:10; 16:12; 18:12; 23:17; Deu 29:19); \"diamond-hard\" hearts (Zec 7:12); Nebuchadnezzar's \"spirit was hardened\" (Dan 5:20); the New Testament hardness of heart (Mat 19:8; Mar 3:5; 6:52; 8:17; 10:5; 16:14; Rom 2:5; Eph 4:18); Psa 95:8 and its New Testament reuse (Heb 3:8, 13, 15; 4:7).", list(range(55, 62)))
S("g72", "God's own seat in grief and compassion, none of it hardened: \"it grieved him to his heart\" (Gen 6:6); \"My heart recoils within me; my compassion grows warm and tender\" (Hos 11:8); \"he became impatient over the misery of Israel\" (Judg 10:16, nephesh short); \"my heart moans for Moab\" (Jer 48:36); \"he does not afflict from his heart\" (Lam 3:33).", list(range(72, 77)))
S("g78", "God's own seat in resolve and purpose, none of it hardened: he \"said in his heart, 'I will never again curse the ground'\" (Gen 8:21); \"what is in my heart\" (1Sa 2:35); \"a man after his own heart\" (1Sa 13:14; Act 13:22); \"my eyes and my heart will be there\" (1Ki 9:3; 2Ch 7:16); 2Ki 10:30; 1Ch 17:19; Psa 33:11; \"the day of vengeance was in my heart\" (Isa 63:4); \"the intents of his heart\" (Jer 23:20; 30:24); \"with all my heart and all my soul\" (Jer 32:41); Job 7:17; 10:13; 34:14.", list(range(78, 87)))
S("g87", "\"It did not come into my heart\" (Jer 7:31; 19:5; 32:35; 44:21): the things never in God's heart.", [87])
S("g89", "God's own seat in delight and aversion, none of it hardened: \"my soul delights\" (Isa 42:1; Mat 12:18); \"his soul hates the wicked\" (Psa 11:5); \"an abomination to his soul\" (Pro 6:16); \"my soul hates ... I am weary of bearing them\" (Isa 1:14); \"I abhor the pride of Jacob\" (Amo 6:8, sworn \"by himself\"); \"my soul has no pleasure in him\" (Heb 10:38); \"my soul will abhor you\" (Lev 26:30; negated in 26:11); \"the beloved of my soul\" handed over (Jer 12:7); the human question \"Does your soul loathe Zion?\" (Jer 14:19).", list(range(89, 98)))
S("jr", "Jer 15:1: \"Though Moses and Samuel stood before me, yet my heart [nephesh, literally 'my soul'] would not turn toward this people. Send them out of my sight.\" God's inner seat refuses a people, and the refusal is proof even against the greatest intercessors; it is not hardening: no hardening word is used, the expression is \"my soul is not toward\" them.", [98, 126])
S("jr2", "Jer 15:1 is probably the text behind the recollection of God's heart hardening: it is the only verse found where God's own seat is turned away; beside it stand the aversion texts Lev 26:30; Psa 11:5; Isa 1:14; Amo 6:8.", [131], basis="read")
S("h104", "Hardness, heaviness and strength are said of God's hand, arm, sword and breath, not of his heart: \"The hand of the Lord was heavy\" (1Sa 5:6, 11); \"his hand is hard against us\" (1Sa 5:7); \"your hand was heavy upon me\" (Psa 32:4); the \"strong hand\" of the Exodus (Exo 6:1; 13:9; Deu 4:34); the \"thick\" rising smoke of his coming in anger (Isa 30:27); \"his hard and great and strong sword\" (Isa 27:1).", [104, 105, 106, 107])
S("h108", "The nearest a hardening word comes to God's own spirit is \"he removed them with his fierce [qasheh, hard] breath [ruach] in the day of the east wind\" (Isa 27:8); read in context this ruach is the east wind as God's instrument, not God's inner spirit; it is recorded as the closest case, not as a positive.", [108], basis="read")
S("h109", "God's heart, when it is described as strong, is strong in understanding, not hard: \"God is mighty ... mighty in strength of heart\" (Job 36:5); \"He is wise in heart and mighty in strength - who has hardened himself against him, and succeeded?\" (Job 9:4): in Job 9:4 God's heart is wise, and the hardening belongs to the human who resists him.", [109])
S("k113", "The same root, used of God, means glory: kaved, \"to be heavy\", is the word for Pharaoh's hardened heart (Exo 8:15, 32; 9:34; 10:1), and in the same narrative God says \"I will harden [chazaq] Pharaoh's heart ... and I will get glory [kaved] over Pharaoh\" (Exo 14:4, 17); the root that makes the human heart heavy makes God glorious (also Lev 10:3; Isa 26:15; Hag 1:8; Eze 28:22; 39:13).", [113, 114, 115], kind="term")
S("d119", "Where a hardening or heaviness word is used of God's own disposition, it is negated: \"He does not retain [chazaq] his anger forever, because he delights in steadfast love\" (Mic 7:18); \"the Lord's hand is not shortened ... or his ear dull [kaved, heavy]\" (Isa 59:1); \"he does not afflict from his heart\" (Lam 3:33); \"my soul shall not abhor you\" (Lev 26:11).", [119, 120, 121, 122])
S("m135", "A human says God is hard, and is wrong: \"Master, I knew you to be a hard man, reaping where you did not sow\" (Mat 25:24), the wicked servant's view of the parable's master, which the parable condemns; in Mal 3:13 the Lord says \"Your words have been hard against me\": the hardness is in the human speech about God.", [135, 136])
S("b160", "The hardening in Psa 95:8, and in its New Testament reuse (Heb 3-4), is human.", [160], verses=["Ps.95.8", "Heb.3.8", "Heb.3.13", "Heb.3.15", "Heb.4.7"])
S("b162", "Hardening runs one way: God hardens and humans harden, but God's own heart is never hardened; where it turns, it turns in compassion (Hos 11:8) or in refusal (Jer 15:1).", [162], basis="read", verses=["Hos.11.8", "Jer.15.1"])
